**Root cause (GAP-DOG-003):** `sessionEntry.Status` was only ever assigned `active`/`cancelled` in `processHandler`/`resultHandler`, so an end decision left the persisted status as `active` and `GET /v1/sessions/{id}` reported `"status": "active"`. The `protocol.SessionCompleted` constant existed but was never written.

**Fix:** in both `processHandler` and `resultHandler`, set `e.Status = protocol.SessionCompleted` when `d.Decision == protocol.DecisionEnd`, **inside the same update block** that records `CurrentDecision` (GAP-009), so status and decision commit atomically and never diverge. `sessionEntry.Status` stays a plain string (consistent with existing code); `getSessionHandler` already normalizes it via `protocol.SessionStatus()`.

```go
// protocol/protocol.go — constants already present; SessionCompleted was unused
const (
	SessionActive    = "active"
	SessionCancelled = "cancelled"
	SessionCompleted = "completed" // now actually persisted on end decisions
)

const (
	DecisionEnd   = "end"
	DecisionCancel = "cancel"
)

// SessionStatus normalizes the stored plain string for the wire.
func SessionStatus(s string) string {
	switch s {
	case SessionCancelled:
		return SessionCancelled
	case SessionCompleted:
		return SessionCompleted
	default:
		return SessionActive // backward-compatible fallback
	}
}
```

```go
// processHandler: one atomic update block (GAP-009 + GAP-DOG-003)
func processHandler(s *store, d Decision) error {
	e := s.get(d.SessionID)
	if e == nil {
		return ErrNotFound
	}
	e.CurrentDecision = d.Decision                    // GAP-009
	if d.Decision == protocol.DecisionEnd {          // GAP-DOG-003
		e.Status = protocol.SessionCompleted
	}
	return s.update(e)                               // single update/transaction
}

// resultHandler: identical lifecycle rules for end decisions on step results
func resultHandler(s *store, d Decision) error {
	e := s.get(d.SessionID)
	if e == nil {
		return ErrNotFound
	}
	e.CurrentDecision = d.Decision                    // GAP-009
	if d.Decision == protocol.DecisionEnd {          // GAP-DOG-003
		e.Status = protocol.SessionCompleted
	}
	return s.update(e)
}
```

`getSessionHandler` needs no change:

```go
func getSessionHandler(s *store, id string) (*sessionResponse, error) {
	e := s.get(id)
	if e == nil {
		return nil, ErrNotFound
	}
	return &sessionResponse{
		ID:              e.ID,
		Status:          protocol.SessionStatus(e.Status), // maps "completed" -> "completed"
		CurrentDecision: e.CurrentDecision,
	}, nil
}
```

**Root cause:** U-GAP-008 replaced stub dispatch with real dispatch (jit `echo` → unwired ⇒ HTTP 501 `NOT_IMPLEMENTED`; `llm` → real provider), but the E2E battery still encoded the *pre-wiring* contract: it asserted the old stub body (`handler_type: jit echo`) and did pool lookups by app **NAME** even though the `:appID` route param is a UUID and rejects names with `404 POOL_NOT_FOUND` by design. The battery wasn't stale about facts — it was asserting a contract that no longer existed.

**Fix 1 — `/execute` jit echo:** replace the body-shape assertion with the designed behavior. Asserting the machine-readable `code` (not free-text `message`) keeps the test robust to message rewording:

```go
// BEFORE (stale) — expected the old stub payload
resp, body := doJSON(t, http.MethodPost, ts.URL+"/execute", map[string]any{
    "handler": "jit", "input": "echo", "payload": map[string]any{"x": 1},
})
if resp.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
    t.Fatalf("status = %d, want 200 (body: %s)", resp.StatusCode, body)
}
var out struct {
    HandlerType string         `json:"handler_type"`
    Echo        map[string]any `json:"echo"`
}
json.Unmarshal(body, &out) // ...
if out.HandlerType != "jit echo" { t.Fatal(...) }

// AFTER (fixed) — designed behavior: HTTP 501 + code NOT_IMPLEMENTED
resp, body := doJSON(t, http.MethodPost, ts.URL+"/execute", map[string]any{
    "handler": "jit", "input": "echo", "payload": map[string]any{"x": 1},
})
expectCode(t, resp, body, http.StatusNotImplemented, CodeNotImplemented)

// shared helper — asserts status AND machine code, immune to message wording
func expectCode(t *testing.T, resp *http.Response, body []byte, wantStatus int, wantCode string) {
    t.Helper()
    if resp.StatusCode != wantStatus {
        t.Fatalf("status = %d, want %d (body: %s)", resp.StatusCode, wantStatus, body)
    }
    var eb struct{ Code string `json:"code"` }
    if err := json.Unmarshal(body, &eb); err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("decode error body %q: %v", body, err)
    }
    if eb.Code != wantCode {
        t.Fatalf("code = %q, want %q", eb.Code, wantCode)
    }
}
```

