```

**Key insight:** The score is `min × width`. To maximize it, you want to keep the window's minimum as high as possible while growing it — so always expand toward the **larger** neighbor. Starting from `[k, k]`, each expansion picks the bigger of `nums[i-1]` and `nums[j+1]`; the running min then degrades as slowly as possible, and every intermediate window is a valid candidate. This runs in **O(n) time, O(1) space**.

**Tricky boundaries to handle:**
1. One side hits the array edge — you must keep expanding the other side (`i == 0` → only `j++`; `j == n-1` → only `i--`).
2. After an expansion, only the newly added element can lower the min — but since the window is always contiguous and contains `k`, updating against both `nums[i]` and `nums[j]` is safe and simple (one is new, the other was already inside).
3. The empty-window case can't occur: the window starts as `[k, k]`, so the answer is always at least `nums[k]`.

```go
// maximumScore returns the max score over all subarrays containing nums[k],
// where score = min(nums[i..j]) * (j-i+1).  O(n) time, O(1) space.
func maximumScore(nums []int, k int) int {
	n := len(nums)
	minVal := nums[k]
	ans := minVal
	i, j := k, k
	for i > 0 || j < n-1 {
		// Expand toward the larger neighbor so minVal falls as slowly as possible.
		if i == 0 {
			j++
		} else if j == n-1 {
			i--
		} else if nums[i-1] < nums[j+1] {
			j++
		} else {
			i--
		}
		// The newly added element is at i or at j; the other index was
		// already inside the window, so it cannot raise minVal.
		if nums[i] < minVal {
			minVal = nums[i]
		}
		if nums[j] < minVal {
			minVal = nums[j]
		}
		if c := minVal * (j - i + 1); c > ans {
			ans = c
		}
	}
	return ans
}
```

**Why the greedy is correct:** The window's min is monotonically non-increasing as it grows. Taking the smaller neighbor first lowers `min` at least as much as taking the larger neighbor first, but cannot ever lead to a *wider* reachable window with the same min — any optimal window `[L, R]` with min `m` is exactly the one the greedy visits when it keeps taking the larger side until that window closes. Every candidate window is evaluated, so the max is captured. (Alternative O(n) formulation: monotonic stack over "nearest smaller element" per index, scoring each index's maximal min-domain that contains `k` — same result, more code.)

The 4 stale rows (auth, assessment, launch, web page — parallel-suite 5s timeouts, filed 2026-08-03) are PRECON-001-class host-contention flakes: the code is green everywhere, only the parallel wall-clock exceeded 5s under load. The gate is immune by design (`--no-file-parallelism`), so these rows can never block a release — which is exactly why they rotted as forever-pending. The fix has two parts:

**1. A closure script that re-runs the flagged files with fresh evidence and closes only on green.**

```ts
// scripts/close-flaky-evidence.ts
// Re-runs flagged files (isolation → parallel batch → full gate) and closes
// the P3 rows only if every run is green. Host-contention flakes self-heal;
// real regressions keep the row open with the failing report attached.
import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
import { writeFileSync, readFileSync, mkdirSync } from "node:fs";

const EVIDENCE_DIR = "artifacts/flaky-evidence";
mkdirSync(EVIDENCE_DIR, { recursive: true });

// The 4 stale rows, mapped to their files.
const ROWS = [
  { id: "EDUOS-4821", files: ["src/api/auth/auth.test.ts"] },            // 15 tests
  { id: "EDUOS-4822", files: ["src/api/assessment/assessment.test.ts"] },// 56 tests
  { id: "EDUOS-4823", files: ["src/api/launch/launch.test.ts"] },        // 15 tests
  { id: "EDUOS-4824", files: ["src/web/page.test.ts"] },                 //  7 tests
];

function vitestRun(files: string[], extra: string[] = []) {
  const out = `${EVIDENCE_DIR}/run-${Date.now()}-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)}.json`;
  const t0 = performance.now();
  execFileSync("npx", ["vitest", "run", "--reporter=json", `--outputFile=${out}`, ...extra, ...files],
    { stdio: "pipe", timeout: 300_000 });
  const report = JSON.parse(readFileSync(out, "utf8"));
  const tests = report.testResults.flatMap((r: any) => r.assertionResults);
  return {
    tests: tests.length,
    passed: tests.filter((t: any) => t.status === "passed").length,
    wallMs: Math.round(performance.now() - t0),
  };
}

// npm test with --no-file-parallelism = the actual guard path.
function gateRun() {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  execFileSync("npm", ["test"], { stdio: "pipe", timeout: 600_000 });
  return { wallMs: Math.round(performance.now() - t0) };
}

const isolation: Record<string, any> = {};
for (const row of ROWS)
  for (const f of row.files)
    isolation[f] = vitestRun([f], ["--no-file-parallelism"]); // (1) each file alone

const apiFiles = ROWS.slice(0, 3).flatMap(r => r.files);
const parallelBatch = vitestRun(apiFiles);                    // (2) the mode that flaked

const gate = gateRun();                                       // (3) full sequential suite

const allGreen =
  Object.values(isolation).every(r => r.tests > 0 && r.passed === r.tests) &&
  parallelBatch.passed === parallelBatch.tests &&
  gate.wallMs >= 0; // npm test exit 0 == green

const evidence = {
  rows: ROWS.map(r => r.id),
  runs: { isolation, parallelBatch, gate },
  verdict: allGreen ? "close" : "keep-open",
  commit: execFileSync("git", ["rev-parse", "HEAD"], { encoding: "utf8" }).trim(),
  host: process.env.CI ? `ci:${process.env.CI_RUNNER}` : `local:${require("os").hostname()}`,
  ts: new Date().toISOString(),
};

writeFileSync(`${EVIDENCE_DIR}/evidence.json`, JSON.stringify(evidence, null, 2));
console.log(JSON.stringify(evidence, null, 2));

if (allGreen) {
  // Idempotent closure by row id + evidence hash; 404 on an already-closed row is a no-op.
  for (const row of ROWS)
    execFileSync("node", ["scripts/eduos-close.mjs", row.id, `${EVIDENCE_DIR}/evidence.json`],
      { stdio: "inherit" });
  console.log(`✓ closed ${ROWS.map(r => r.id).join(", ")} with fresh evidence`);
} else {
  console.error(`✗ kept ${ROWS.map(r => r.id).join(", ")} open — attach ${EVIDENCE_DIR}/evidence.json`);
  process.exit(1);
}
```

**2. Hardening so this class of row stops being filed at all (triage-before-filing).**

```ts
// scripts/triage-flaky.ts — run BEFORE filing a new flaky row. Generates the
// same isolation/parallel/gate evidence at filing time, so a row is only ever
// filed when a run genuinely fails; self-healing contention never becomes a row.
export async function triage(files: string[]): Promise<"flake" | "regression"> {
  const isolation = files.map(f => vitestRun([f], ["--no-file-parallelism"]));
  const parallel = vitestRun(files);                 // the mode that timed out
  const allGreen = [...isolation, parallel].every(r => r.passed === r.tests);
  return allGreen ? "flake" : "regression";          // flake → close in tracker, no row
}
```

Anti-patterns explicitly **not** used: no blanket `testTimeout: 30000`, no `retry: 3` on the gate, no `--poolOptions.threads.singleThread` hack. Those would mask real regressions. The parallel timeout stays at 5s; the closure evidence is what changes.

**Root cause (DSD-GAP-018):** both the per-file error toast and the final summary toast were written to the *same* `#toast` container, so the summary (`Done: 0 succeeded, 1 failed`) clobbered the per-file message. Users saw counts but never *why* a file failed.

**Fix (4 parts, implemented in `src/uploader.js`):**

1. **`handleMultipleUpload` collects `failReasons`** as `` `${file.name}: ${e.message}` `` per failing file.
2. **Final summary toast appends the reasons**, truncated to 200 chars, and is flagged `warn: true` when any file failed — the detail can no longer be overwritten/lost.
3. **`_processSingleFile` throws a specific, actionable error** (`No amount-*/cost-* CSV found in archive`) instead of a generic unzip failure.
4. **Restored the DOM-writing toast stub** (writes real `textContent`/`dataset` into `#toast`) so end-to-end tests can assert exactly what the user sees.

```js
// handleMultipleUpload — key changed lines
const failReasons = [];
for (const file of list) {
  try {
    results.push({ file: file.name, ok: true, data: await _processSingleFile(file) });
  } catch (e) {
    const reason = `${file.name}: ${e.message}`;          // (1) collect reason
    failReasons.push(reason);
    results.push({ file: file.name, ok: false, error: e.message });
    toast({ type: "error", message: truncateReason(reason, reasonLimit), warn: true });
  }
}
const okCount = results.filter(r => r.ok).length;
const failCount = list.length - okCount;

let summary = `Done: ${okCount} succeeded, ${failCount} failed`;
if (failReasons.length) summary += " — " + truncateReason(failReasons.join("; "), reasonLimit);
toast({ type: failCount ? "error" : "success",
        message: truncateReason(summary, reasonLimit),    // (2) counts + reasons, ≤200 chars
        warn: failCount > 0 });
return { ok: failCount === 0, results, failReasons };
```

```js
// _processSingleFile — specific error (3)
if (!amountCsv && !costCsv) {
  throw new Error("No amount-*/cost-* CSV found in archive");
}
```

```js
// truncateReason (shared helper)
export function truncateReason(text, limit = 200) {
  if (!text) return "";
  return text.length <= limit ? text : text.slice(0, limit - 3).trimEnd() + "...";
}
```

